          }
        },
        "entry_tWK43t": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When do I know if I can repot my plant?",
            "text": "<p>Your plant will give you a few clear signs! Look out for roots growing out from the drainage hole, soil has sinked considerably and an increase in frequency of watering. As a general rule, most plants love a slightly bigger home every 1 or 2 years.<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "entry_NRyCrx": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Should I trim my plant?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, absolutely! Trimming\/pruning is like giving a fresh haircut to your plant. It encourages the plant to grow back fuller and helps to neaten up the plant.<\/p><p>Always snip off any leaves that have completely turned yellow, brown, or crispy. These won't turn green again, and removing them signals the plant to redirect its energy into growing shiny new leaves instead.<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "entry_k9TMrz": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I need to fertilise my plant regularly?",
            "text": "<p>Not necessarily! Fertilisers are more of a vitamin boost rather than actual food (plants make their own food using sunlight!). But if you want to accelerate its growth and see bigger, lusher leaves, a little fertiliser can definitely give it that extra push. Otherwise, your plant can and will do perfectly fine without it.<\/p><p>Never fertilise a plant that is undergoing stress, wilting, or root rot. Fertiliser is meant to boost a healthy plant, not rescue a sick one!<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "entry_CTi6tP": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How often should I water my plant?",
            "text": "<p>Watering frequency varies depending on the plant, potting mix, and lighting environment. We recommend checking the soil moisture with a chopstick before watering or refer to the plant’s care guide on our website!<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "entry_GB9aym": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I overwater my plant?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, it’s possible. Overwatering is one of the most common causes of unhealthy plants. Always allow the potting mix to reach the recommended moisture level before the next watering cycle.<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "entry_bBXFk7": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My plant dropped a few leaves. Is this normal?",
            "text": "<p>Yes. It's common for plants to experience minor leaf drop after transport or when adjusting to its new environment. Given time and proper care, most plants will recover and begin producing new growth.<\/p>"
          }
        }
      },
